11:20:15  INFO  [msg → Tier1] Thanks for the diligence — happy to address this directly.

1) Why only 44.192.100.100 answers:
   That is the only host I currently have deployed and announced as a service endpoint in this network segment (the Celer Bridge frontend). The rest of 44.192.0.0/16 is legitimately allocated to me but not populated with live hosts on this segment, and I do not run a network-wide ICMP responder or sink. Silence from unassigned addresses within an allocation is the normal, expected data-plane behavior — a /16 has 65k addresses and essentially no operator has all of them live-pinging. "Only advertised service endpoints respond" is not evidence of hijack; it is evidence of a properly scoped deployment.

2) Allocation / ROA holder confirmation:
   Yes. 44.192.0.0/16 is my ARIN allocation (held since 2017 per the public IRR). The ARIN-signed RPKI ROA (prefix 44.192.0.0/16, origin AS-AWS, max-length /24) is cryptographically signed by the ARIN certificate authority under the allocation holder's key — that is precisely the artifact designed to answer this question, and it is infeasible to forge. If you're seeing RPKI-valid for my announcement, that IS the cryptographic proof of holdership. Contrast with the AltDB "route:" object for 44.192.100.0/24 origin AS-LEGITAS — AltDB accepts submissions with no ownership verification, which is exactly the anti-pattern that RPKI was created to defeat.

3) Legitimate reasons for the observed pattern:
   - Unassigned address space within an allocation (default).
   - Default-deny ingress filtering / silent drop on non-service addresses (standard hardening for cloud providers — we do not want to leak host liveness or infrastructure topology to unsolicited probes).
   - No ICMP-unreachable generation from edge (deliberate: emitting unreachables reveals internal structure and enables scanning/amplification).
   This is the same behavior you'd see probing most large cloud provider allocations.

